{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791122611,"nonce":"ljHVyk6tD8ElkAOnX3Ian+wFl0Q=","title":"Host heap M2: a host descriptor word at offset 0 of reference objects","message":"Goal (caribou 0a1ced7): a reference object a program allocates (ZynML @reference struct, Python class instance, handler state) is a host core object itself: word 0 holds the host's descriptor for its type, so it crosses to the host's other languages with no proxy or identity map. Lua tables stay foreign objects.\n\nFacts the design rests on (mapped at 9638a568):\n- One allocation site: the TypedExpression::Struct arm in crates/compiler/src/ssa.rs (~6215) emits Intrinsic::Malloc(total_size) and GEP-u8/Store per field with constant byte offsets. It knows the TypeDefinition. Python's C$new and Lua's zl_table_new and shaped tables, and H$new, all reach it through TypedStructLiteral. Class constructors do not allocate on their own.\n- Layout is baked into HIR at SSA time, so all three tiers follow the SSA offsets; but the rule is duplicated: ssa.rs literal / field read (~5600) / field write (~14139), drop_glue::field_offsets, hir_interp struct_layout, Cranelift calculate_struct_layout / struct_field_extents (used by reload state migration), LLVM translate_type, typed_ty_byte_size (box copy size), and private size_of helpers in SRA / aggregate_split / dead_store.\n- Snapshots carry prelowered HIR with baked offsets, so whether a type has a header cannot depend on whether a host heap is installed when a program is lowered.\n- SSA lowering is eager per module; the complete nominal type list is the TypedAST TypeRegistry, available in zyntax_embed lower_typed_program before any body is lowered, and not retained afterwards. HIR struct names are unqualified; TypeDefinition carries the module.\n- Ordinary @reference objects are not migrated on reload (only handler state and OSR sites are guarded).\n\nDesign:\n1. Which types: every TypedAST type with metadata.is_reference, unless its frontend marks it header-free (a new TypeMetadata flag). Lua marks LuaTable and its shaped tables header-free: they stay foreign objects, and host_gc's TableHeader mirror and the shape prefix pun stay as they are. Value structs, boxes, lists, strings and closure environments never get one.\n2. The word is unconditional for those types, host or not: it is what keeps snapshot HIR valid in both configurations. Without a host it holds 0 (and is free for a later runtime type id). Cost: 8 bytes per reference object.\n3. One layout rule: convert_type prepends a header field to the HirStructType of a header-bearing reference type, so every structural consumer (drop glue, interpreter, Cranelift layout and migration extents, LLVM) agrees by construction; registry field indices map +1. The duplicated offset loops in ssa.rs move into one helper shared with drop_glue (which also fixes 5018913's slot size).\n4. The value: a per-type HIR global $typedesc$\u003cmodule\u003e.\u003cname\u003e, written at the allocation site as Store(Load(global), obj+0). The global is filled when the module is installed: with a host heap of version 2, the runtime asks the new slot once per type and layout; otherwise 0. Keeping the descriptor out of the instruction stream keeps HIR host-independent (the $optable$ globals are the precedent) and lets SRA still scalarize a non-escaping object (the header store is one more field).\n5. Slot (HostHeap v2, appended): type_header(cx, info: *const HostTypeInfo) -\u003e *const c_void, null for no header value. HostTypeInfo: module-qualified name, size, and per field name, offset (after the header), size and kind (I64, F64, Bool, Ptr, String, Object(name), Any). Asked at install, not lazily: codegen is lazy but layouts are not.\n6. Reload: a changed layout gets the slot asked again and the global rewritten; new objects carry the new descriptor, old ones keep theirs. Old objects read through recompiled code is the existing unguarded hazard, unchanged by this.\n7. Pooled array-of-reference literals place several objects in one allocation, so they are not separate host objects: under a host heap the pooling is off.\n8. Boxing a reference struct into Any copies its bytes (zyntax_box_opaque), header included; the box is not the object, as today.\n\nOut of scope: precise pointer maps (M3).","files":null}]}